.\" Manpage for socksy.
.TH SOCKSY 1 "2026-08-08" "socksy 0.5.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
socksy \- the easy way to run an authenticated SOCKS5 proxy system-wide on Linux desktops
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B socksy
.I command
.RI [ options ]
.RI [ 'user:pass@host:port' ]
.PP
A proxy may be given as
.IR user:pass@host:port ,
with an explicit scheme
.RI ( socks5://... ", " http://... ", " https://... ),
as the
.I host:port:user:pass
form that most providers export, or as a bare
.I host:port
when no authentication is needed.
.SH DESCRIPTION
A desktop's system proxy settings cannot send a SOCKS username and password.
.B socksy
works around this by running a small local relay
.RB ( gost )
that injects your credentials and forwards traffic upstream, then points the
system proxy at the local relay
.RB ( 127.0.0.1:1081
by default).
.PP
The system proxy is set through a backend chosen for your environment:
.B gnome
.RB ( gsettings ),
.B kde
.RI ( ~/.config/kioslaverc
via
.BR kwriteconfig ),
or
.B env
(a sourceable
.IR ~/.config/socksy/env.sh
for CLI-only or other desktops). The backend is autodetected and can be
overridden with
.B SOCKSY_BACKEND
or the
.B backend
config key.
.PP
Everything lives under
.I ~/.local
and
.IR ~/.config ;
no root is required. The relay runs as a systemd user service, so it survives
logout and restarts on failure.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.BI "set [--type " t "] [--country " XX "] [--sticky|--session " id "] '" proxy "'"
Apply a proxy now. Installs gost on first use and auto-tests the exit IP.
.TP
.B on
Re-apply the last proxy.
.TP
.B off
Turn the proxy off (back to a direct connection).
.TP
.BI "rotate " [id]
Re-apply the last proxy under a fresh sticky-session tag, which is how a
rotating provider is asked for a new exit IP. Any existing session tag is
replaced rather than stacked; the country tag, type, and credentials are kept.
With
.I id
the tag is set to that value instead of a random one.
.TP
.BI "run [--profile " name "] " "command " [ args... ]
Run a single command with the standard
.IR *_proxy
environment variables pointed at the relay, leaving the desktop's system proxy
untouched. Without
.B --profile
the running relay is reused, and is started from the last proxy if none is up.
With
.B --profile
a throwaway relay is started on its own port and torn down when the command
exits, so it never disturbs a system-wide relay other applications are using.
The command's exit status is passed through.
.TP
.BR "status " [ --json ]
Show what is active. With
.B --json
print machine-readable output.
.TP
.B test
Print the current exit IP.
.TP
.BR "check " [ --json "] [" --strict ]
Audit for leaks.
.B status
reports what socksy configured;
.B check
probes what actually happens and prints one row per finding: whether the relay
is listening and bound to loopback only, whether the system proxy points at it,
where requests really exit, whether the machine reaches that same address with
no proxy at all (which would mean nothing is being proxied), whether a direct
IPv6 route or a public bypass entry routes around the relay, whether Firefox
still resolves names locally, whether the exit matches a
.B --country
tag, and whether the upstream credentials are visible in
.BR ps .
Exits non-zero if any check failed, or with
.B --strict
on any warning as well.
.B --json
prints the whole audit as one document for scripts and CI.
.TP
.BI "watch " [seconds]
Live exit-IP loop (default 5s); flags every change.
.TP
.BR "watchdog " { on | off | status }
Auto-restart the relay when the exit goes bad (systemd user timer).
.TP
.BI "logs [-f] [-n " lines ]
Show the relay's journal (default 50 lines).
.B -f
follows it.
.TP
.BR "dns " { on | off | reset | status }
Remote DNS for Firefox (stops DNS leaks). CLI tools using socks5h and Chrome
already resolve remotely.
.B off
pins the pref to false;
.B reset
instead removes socksy's managed block from
.I user.js
entirely, deleting the file if socksy created it and nothing else is left.
.TP
.BR "bypass " { list | add | rm | reset }
Manage the no-proxy
.RB ( ignore-hosts )
list.
.TP
.BI "save " "name '" proxy "'"
Remember a proxy under a name.
.TP
.BR "use " [ name ]
Apply a saved proxy (no name = last used).
.TP
.B list
List saved proxies (passwords masked).
.TP
.BI "rm " name
Forget a saved proxy.
.SH SET FLAGS
.TP
.BI "--type " t
Upstream type:
.BR socks5 " (default), " http ", or " https .
.TP
.BI "--country " XX
Append a country tag to the username, e.g.
.BR "--country GR" .
.TP
.B --sticky
Append a random session tag so your exit IP stays put.
.TP
.BI "--session " id
Reuse a specific session tag (same id = same IP window).
.SH CONFIG FILE
.I ~/.config/socksy/config
holds optional
.RI ' "key = value" '
defaults (parsed, never executed). Recognised keys:
.BR local_port ", " gost_version ", " gost_path ", " session_keyword ", "
.BR country_keyword ", " type ", " ignore_hosts ", " health_interval ", "
.BR health_fails ", " health_url ", " backend ", " relay_creds ", " ip_url ", "
.BR ipv6_url ", " geo_url .
Precedence is environment variable, then config file, then built-in default.
.TP
.BR ip_url ", " ipv6_url ", " geo_url
Endpoints used to observe an exit.
.B ip_url
and
.B ipv6_url
must echo the caller's address back as plain text;
.B geo_url
must answer with a two-letter country code. All three may be pointed at a
self-hosted equivalent instead of the public defaults.
.TP
.B relay_creds
How the upstream credentials reach
.BR gost :
.B file
hands it a
.B 600
config file,
.B args
passes them with
.B -F
on its command line, and
.B auto
(the default) uses
.B file
where the installed gost supports it. See
.B SECURITY
below.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B SOCKSY_LOCAL_PORT
Local relay listen port (default 1081).
.TP
.B SOCKSY_SESSION_KEYWORD ", " SOCKSY_COUNTRY_KEYWORD
Provider-specific tag keywords (defaults
.IR -session- " / " -country- ).
.TP
.B SOCKSY_TYPE
Default upstream type.
.TP
.B SOCKSY_HEALTH_INTERVAL ", " SOCKSY_HEALTH_FAILS ", " SOCKSY_HEALTH_URL
Watchdog probe cadence, failure threshold, and probe URL.
.TP
.B SOCKSY_BACKEND
Desktop proxy backend:
.BR gnome ", " kde ", or " env
(default: autodetected).
.TP
.B SOCKSY_GOST
Absolute path to the
.B gost
binary to use, instead of letting socksy find or download one.
.TP
.B SOCKSY_RELAY_CREDS
How credentials reach the relay:
.BR file ", " args ", or " auto
(default).
.TP
.B SOCKSY_IP_URL ", " SOCKSY_IPV6_URL ", " SOCKSY_GEO_URL
Endpoints used by
.BR check " (and " test ")"
to observe an exit.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.local/bin/socksy
The command, for a rootless install.
.I /usr/bin/socksy
when installed from a distro package.
.TP
.I ~/.local/bin/gost
The relay binary, when socksy downloaded it. A
.B gost
already present on
.B PATH
(for example from a distro package) is used instead.
.TP
.I ~/.config/systemd/user/socksy-relay.service
The relay service.
.TP
.I ~/.config/systemd/user/socksy-watchdog.{service,timer}
The health watchdog (opt-in).
.TP
.I ~/.config/socksy/relay.yaml
The relay's config, holding the upstream credentials (mode
.BR 600 ).
.TP
.I ~/.config/socksy/
Config, saved profiles, and state.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Apply an authenticated proxy:
.PP
.RS
.EX
socksy set 'user123:secret@eu.example.net:1080'
.EE
.RE
.PP
Geo-targeted, sticky exit:
.PP
.RS
.EX
socksy set --country GR --sticky 'user123:secret@eu.example.net:1080'
.EE
.RE
.PP
Turn on the health watchdog:
.PP
.RS
.EX
socksy watchdog on
.EE
.RE
.PP
Paste a provider's own line, then ask for a new exit IP:
.PP
.RS
.EX
socksy set 'eu.example.net:1080:user123:secret'
socksy rotate
.EE
.RE
.PP
Proxy a single application, leaving the desktop direct:
.PP
.RS
.EX
socksy run --profile work firefox
.EE
.RE
.PP
Audit the current posture, treating warnings as failures:
.PP
.RS
.EX
socksy check --strict
.EE
.RE
.SH SECURITY
Proxy credentials are stored in plain text in the relay config, the systemd
unit, and the profiles file, all written with mode
.BR 600 .
.PP
Credentials are also kept off the relay's command line. Passing them as
.I gost -F socks5://user:pass@host:port
would place the password in
.IR /proc/<pid>/cmdline ,
which is world-readable on a default Linux kernel, so any local user could read
it out of
.BR ps (1)
while the relay ran, whatever the file permissions. socksy instead hands
.B gost
a
.B 600
config file.
.B socksy status
reports the mode in use; the
.B relay_creds
config key forces it either way, and a gost too old to accept a generated config
falls back to the command line with a warning.
.PP
socksy never sends your credentials anywhere except to the upstream proxy you
specify.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR gsettings (1),
.BR systemctl (1),
.BR curl (1)
.PP
